# Beat for demo step "Hidden bits": the covert channel reveal (spec 0396).
#
# Drives protolens through one captured *response* that carries "whoami" in
# the hidden channel. The bits ride on the grid's `cells` varints; with a
# grid of at least 20x20, the first row's cells 1 and 2 are the start of the
# `w` (01110111) — cell 1 canonical, cell 2 the first spurious continuation
# byte. The beat opens on the whole message, then shows the two contiguous
# cells side by side at the wire level, highlighting each in turn, and
# finally decodes the hidden bits. Advance with space or Enter; the
# presenter may also move by hand.
#
# Step text is written for a 120-column line (spec 0400).
#
# Invocation (from grehack2026.sh), on the flagged response capture:
#   protolens --descriptor-set life.desc capture/NNNNNN-response.pb \
#       --script beats/smuggle

title: The smuggled bits

steps:
  - text: |
      One captured response, in full. protolens flags fields that do
      not sit the way the schema expects — spurious padding on varints.

      standard tools see nothing: the padding does not change the decoded value.

      👋 Press space to step onto the first two cells.
    node: /

  - text: |
      The grid is just cells. Here are the first row's first two cells, at the wire
      level (`w`). The first — highlighted — is a plain, minimal varint: nothing hidden.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/1
    wire_lines:
      from: /1/1/1
      to: /1/1/2
    search: /1/1/1

  - text: |
      The second cell — now highlighted — decodes to the same value, but the wire shows an extra byte: a
      continuation byte (high bit set) then a zero. Legal, non-minimal, and that extra byte carries one hidden bit.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/2
    wire_lines:
      from: /1/1/1
      to: /1/1/2
    search: /1/1/2

  - text: |
      Eight cells, eight bits — the first byte, selected end to end. The bytes are lowercase ASCII: 01110111 → w.

        ch dec binary    ch dec binary   ch dec binary    ch dec binary   ch dec binary
        a   97 01100001  b  98 01100010  c   99 01100011  d  100 01100100  e  101 01100101
        f  102 01100110  g 103 01100111  h  104 01101000  i  105 01101001  j  106 01101010
        k  107 01101011  l 108 01101100  m  109 01101101  n  110 01101110  o  111 01101111
        p  112 01110000  q 113 01110001  r  114 01110010  s  115 01110011  t  116 01110100
        u  117 01110101  v 118 01110110  w  119 01110111  x  120 01111000  y  121 01111001
        z  122 01111010

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/1
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/1
      to: /1/1/8

  - text: |
      The next eight cells → 01101000 → h.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/9
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/9
      to: /1/1/16

  - text: |
      The next eight → 01101111 → o.

      👋 Press space.
    node: /1/1/17
    select_lines:
      from: /1/1/17
      to: /1/1/24

  - text: |
      The cells we just read start the first byte:

        01110111 01101000 01101111 01100001 01101101 01101001
           w        h        o        a        m        i      → whoami

      This is a response, so it carries Eve's command. The matching request carries Bob's answer, "experiment", the same way.

      👋 Type `:q` to quit protolens.
